americans have gotten so divided along  political lines in recent years it's  like we're not even living in the same  country we're the same time period  literally and that's not just impacting  your social media feed data scientists  have found that this is starting to  influence major economic data points  i'll break down how we know just how big  the partisan divide in the u.s is how  this is affecting economic reports that  policy makers rely on and how this could  be affecting the u.s economy let's get  into it  data show that americans have high  savings less debt and there are  literally more job openings than at any  time in history all signs of a healthy  growing economy but we found out that  u.s consumer sentiment in february was  the lowest it's been in more than a  decade so what gives this may have more  to do with perception  than reality a recent report from the  university of michigan finds that  partisan views now dominate consumers  economic expectations  and perceptions of the economy between  republicans and democrats are the most  divergent they've ever been the readings  have reached a staggering new level  under the past two presidents a 52-point  gap between republicans and democrats to  put that number into perspective the  difference between how americans rated  the economy at its highest point in the  university of michigan survey  when the u.s was booming during early  2000  and when we were at its lowest point  during the inflation-driven recession of  1980 was only 46 points republicans and  democrats today have more different  opinions of the economy than americans  who were in the middle of an economic  boom and those who were suffering  through a staggering recession here's  how that's impacting the economic data  that policy makers rely on listen  confidence matters  investors retailers manufacturers banks  the government they all look to this  confidence data to give them clues about  what's coming next in the economy this  also matters for inflation expectations  for inflation are one of the biggest  factors in actual or realized inflation  and right now in the university of  michigan survey we're seeing republicans  expectations of inflation  way above democrats expectations of  inflation in fact republicans  expectations may be too high while  democrats expectations may be too low  here's how this is affecting the u.s  economy weak consumer confidence has  historically led to a decline in  consumer spending and we've seen that  over the last couple months if that  continues that can lead to lower  consumption which drags economic growth  lower that's especially important in a  consumer-driven economy like the united  states businesses look at these consumer  sentiment readings too low consumer  confidence numbers can cause  manufacturers to decrease their  inventories in advance they might even  delay investing in new projects or  facilities banks see these numbers and  they can prepare for reductions in  lending like mortgages and credit cards  now none of that is to say that there  aren't real issues hurting consumer  sentiment right now i spoke to the man  who literally wrote the book on reading  economic indicators bernard bommel chief  economist the economic outlet group he  tells me that the depressing impact of  covid19's omicron variant and the rising  cost of living have had a notable impact  on sentiment those are the real factors  he says everything else is kind of fluff  but fluff is moving the needle this  hyper partisanship can lead to a vicious  circle of negative outcomes for both  sides according to richard curtin who  put together the university of  michigan's report richard tells me that  democrats are more likely to make poor  investment decisions because they think  the economy is better than it is and  take too much risk republicans with low  confidence are more likely to not invest  in things like starting a business  because they think the economy is worse  than it actually is as we've seen when  it comes to inflation confidence and  investment  perception impacts reality in curtains  words people have moved from economic  evaluations to political evaluations and  that's making the job of economists and  investors that much harder  if you enjoyed this video hit the like  button and if you're interested in  markets make sure to subscribe as the  elder chuck d once said they claim that  i'm a criminal by now i wonder how some  people never know the enemy could be  their friend guardian in short don't  believe the hype  i'll see you next time
 

